MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-7-/// <summary>
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-8-///     A cache-only open adopts a block despite a lost journal checkpoint rather than failing
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-9-///     the drive (see <see cref="FileIndexCheckpointLossLifetimeTests" />), because a cache-only
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-10-///     open never watches and the block is still a correct snapshot as of its age. But starting a
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-11-///     live watch from that block's cursor would resume from a position the journal no longer
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-12-///     holds, so <see cref="FileIndex.StartWatchingAsync(char, CancellationToken)" /> refuses such
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs:13:///     a drive, says why, and requests no watch. A successful <see cref="FileIndex.RescanAsync(char, CancellationToken)" />
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-14-///     writes a fresh cursor and clears the refusal, leaving the drive ready to start. The journal
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-15-///     read is swapped out through <c>JournalCheckpointCheck.OverrideJournalForTest</c>, so these
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-16-///     run on every platform and never touch a real volume.
MFTLib.Tests/Index\FileIndexCacheOnlyUnresumableWatchTests.cs-17-/// </summary>
