docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-33-6. Failure policy: a per-drive fault (journal wrapped, drive removed, a batch that cannot be applied) recovers by rescanning that drive; connection-level uncertainty closes that drive's channel and fails loudly. A channel is never repaired.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-34-7. Liveness: known failures are reported by an explicit signal; heartbeats, a host watchdog and request timeouts are the backstop. `Stalled` is its own frame kind and is reported as a loud `Channel` fault with no recovery rescan.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-35-8. Concurrent scans are permitted and limited to no more than one scan per core. It is enforced by amendment S3 below: the broker owns one parse thread per processor, every running scan holds at least one, and a scan that cannot get one waits. This replaces the spec's separate scan cap and per-call thread budget.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-36-9. One diagnostics log per process, each line tagged by channel.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md:37:10. `JournalBrokerScanSession` and `ScanSessionTestHarness` are deleted, not reshaped; `BrokerTestHarness` replaces the harness.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-38-11. The spec's two readings are binding: a scan runs on its own drive pipe with only the channel-open handshake on the control pipe; a recovery rescan keeps the drive's `LiveWatch` checkpoint-loss report.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-39-12. [MFTLib issue 264 (0.3.0 ships no TestExtensions package)](https://gitea.fleet.sticktoitive.net/schoen/MFTLib/issues/264): ship `MFTLib.TestExtensions` as its own nuget.org package pinned to the exact `MFTLib` version.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-40-13. Every place drives are scanned is concurrent and limited by core count through the broker's one parse-thread allocator, with no thread count passed by any caller: open, batched rescan, single-drive rescan, recovery rescan. `FileIndex.OpenAsync` scans its drives concurrently; `FileIndexOptions.OpenProgress` reports each drive as it finishes, from whichever thread finished it, and both consumers' progress handlers migrate. The impact is measured (M1). (Owner, 2026-09-28: "I thought we settled on concurrent, limited by core count, and we'd measure the impact.")
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-per-drive-watch-channels.md-41-14. A running scan's parse-thread count is rebalanced at every chunk ([owner ruling 6 on MFTLib issue 265](https://gitea.fleet.sticktoitive.net/schoen/MFTLib/issues/265#issuecomment-97869)). The broker owns the process-wide budget of one parse thread per processor and divides it among running scans; no caller computes a share, and no thread count crosses the wire or the producer contract.
--
